# Build context trim for `docker build -f docker/Dockerfile .` (root context, whole reactor).
#
# docker/Dockerfile deliberately does `COPY . .` rather than listing modules: the reactor builds
# service's dependency from source (`events` is never published), and a module list would have to be
# edited every time a module is added. This file is what makes that cheap — it drops everything the
# build does not need, so adding a module stays free and no stale output leaks in.

.git
.gitignore

# Maven / Quarkus build output — regenerated inside the build stage, and hundreds of megabytes of it
# here. Copying it in would also risk a stale target/ shadowing what the stage compiles.
**/target
**/target-ide

# The webui submodule's install output: a symlink farm full of platform-native binaries compiled for
# the host rather than for ubi9, and nothing in the build stage reads it — the stage runs no install
# and no client build (see docker/Dockerfile). `.angular` is the CLI's own cache, the same shape of
# host-local junk.
#
# NOTE THE ASYMMETRY with the platform's Quinoa recipe
# (docs/project-setup-quinoa-angular.md in the qits superproject), which excludes the client's dist/
# as well: here dist/ is the PAYLOAD. qits-spa-events depends on @qits/ui-components, which exists
# only on the platform's own npm registry, reachable from no address a docker build has — so the
# bundle is built BEFORE this image by .config/qits/ci-post-receive.yml, on qits-net where that
# registry answers. Excluding dist/ would fail the build at the `test -f` guard on the mvnw line.
# Every SPA-serving service in the platform carries this same departure, for the same reason.
**/node_modules
service/src/main/webui/.angular

# Local env / IDE cruft. `.env` is per-machine Quarkus overrides read at ordinal 295 — above the
# packaged application.properties — so shipping one into the build context would let a developer's
# local port quietly outrank what the image is supposed to run with.
.env
.idea
.vscode
.settings
.sdkmanrc

# Docs and the docker/ dir itself: nothing in the build reads them, and keeping them out means
# editing this Dockerfile does not bust the `COPY . .` layer. `events/` is a MODULE, not prose — it
# stays.
*.md
docs
docker
